A set named after a predeclared identifier is rejected even when the generated
function's own signature never mentions it. //plumb:new generates func new(), whose
signature is () *Server and references "new" nowhere, yet a top-level func new
shadows the builtin for the whole package, so the sibling's new(Server) below would
stop compiling. (The guard fires whenever a top-level name shadows a predeclared
identifier for the whole package, not only when the generated signature mentions
the name.)
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
output: plumb_gen.go
error-is: ErrShadowsPredeclared
-- example.com/app/sibling.go --
package app

var ptr = new(Server)
-- example.com/app/a.go --
package app

type Server struct{}

//plumb:new
func NewServer() *Server { return &Server{} }
